text:
A configuration that is only these ideas
terraform {
  required_version = "~&gt; 1.13.0"

  required_providers {
    random = {
      source  = "hashicorp/random"
      version = "~&gt; 3.6"
    }
  }
}

resource "random_pet" "service" {
  length = 2
}

output "name" {
  value = random_pet.service.id
}
tofu init, tofu plan, and tofu apply against that file are the whole execution model with no account attached.

For Expressions: Transforming Collections Elegantly
A for expression builds a new collection. It does not loop at apply time. It is evaluated while the configuration is being built.
locals {
  ports = [80, 443]

  listeners = [
    for port in local.ports : {
      port     = port
      protocol = port == 443 ? "https" : "http"
    }
  ]

  by_protocol = {
    for listener in local.listeners : listener.protocol =&gt; listener.port...
  }
}
The trailing ... groups duplicates into a list. Without it, duplicate keys are an error. A filter clause keeps some elements: for port in local.ports : port if port &gt; 100.
for_each on a resource wants a set of strings or a map. A list of objects is the wrong shape until you turn it into a map keyed by a stable name. Keying by list index means a deletion in the middle recreates every later instance. Key by a name the human owns.

Dynamic Blocks: Generating Repetitive Configuration Programmatically
Some resources take repeated nested blocks (ingress, statement). You cannot put for_each on a nested block. dynamic generates the blocks from a collection.
locals {
  ingress_ports = [22, 443]
}

# Sketch. The security-group type is the provider's, not part of the pet lab.
# resource "aws_security_group" "edge" {
#   dynamic "ingress" {
#     for_each = toset(local.ingress_ports)
#     content {
#       from_port   = ingress.value
#       to_port     = ingress.value
#       protocol    = "tcp"
#       cidr_blocks = ["203.0.113.10/32"]
#     }
#   }
# }
Inside content, ingress.value is the element and ingress.key is the key. Name the dynamic block after the nested block type the provider expects, not after a name you wish the provider had used.
Dynamic blocks hide structure. Three nearly identical blocks written out are easier to review than a dynamic block over a clever local. Use dynamic when the collection is data that changes size. Write the blocks when there are two of them and a human should see both.

Input Variables: Types, Defaults, Validation Rules, and Sensitive Values
variable "pet_length" {
  description = "Words in the generated name."
  type        = number
  default     = 2

  validation {
    condition     = var.pet_length &gt;= 1 && var.pet_length &lt;= 4
    error_message = "pet_length must be from 1 to 4."
  }
}

variable "name_prefix" {
  description = "Optional prefix. Null means no prefix."
  type        = string
  default     = null
  nullable    = true
}
type is what stops a string from arriving where a number belongs. default makes the variable optional. No default means the caller must supply it. description is the documentation. nullable = false rejects an explicit null when a default exists and null would be a mistake.
validation runs on the value the caller passed. It can see var.pet_length. It cannot see other resources’ computed attributes in a useful way, and it should not try. Validate shape and policy here: length, prefix, membership in a list. Validate the live infrastructure with a plan or a test, not with a variable block.
sensitive = true marks the value so plans redact it. The value is still in state and still in memory. Sensitivity is a display flag. Chapter 12’s rule stands: do not put the long-lived credential in a variable that gets stored at all if you can pass it only to a provider from the environment.

Locals: Organizing Complex Logic and Avoiding Repetition
Locals are names for expressions. They are not inputs. Callers cannot set them.
locals {
  prefix    = var.name_prefix == null ? "" : "${var.name_prefix}-"
  pet_words = var.pet_length
}

resource "random_pet" "service" {
  length = local.pet_words
  prefix = local.prefix == "" ? null : trimsuffix(local.prefix, "-")
}
Use a local when the same expression appears twice, or when a conditional is too long to sit inside a resource. Do not use a local as a second, undocumented input. If a caller should change it, it is a variable.
Locals can refer to resources. That reference is unknown until apply when the resource is being created, and everything downstream inherits the unknown. Keep locals that feed for_each keys on variables and static data.

Outputs: Exposing Information and Connecting Modules
output "name" {
  description = "Generated pet name."
  value       = random_pet.service.id
}

output "state_passphrase_in_use" {
  description = "Never the passphrase. Only whether one was set."
  value       = var.state_passphrase != null
  sensitive   = false
}
Outputs are the root module’s result (tofu output) and the child module’s return values (module.pets.name). Mark an output sensitive = true when its value is sensitive, or OpenTofu will refuse to let a sensitive value flow into it. The flag still does not encrypt the output. Root outputs end up available to anyone who can read the state.
Export the minimum: an id, an ARN, a name. Do not export an entire resource object. Callers will depend on attributes you did not mean to support, and a provider upgrade will break them.

Passing Data Between Modules: The Parent-Child Contract
A parent passes variables. A child returns outputs. That is the whole contract.
module "pets" {
  source     = "./modules/pets"
  pet_length = 2
}

output "pet_name" {
  value = module.pets.name
}
The parent does not read module.pets.random_pet.service.id. That path reaches into the child’s resources, couples the parent to names that are none of its business, and breaks the day the child renames a resource. If the parent needs the value, the child grows an output.
The same rule applies sideways. Two root modules do not read each other’s variables. They read each other’s outputs through remote state (chapter 7), and only the outputs. Remote state is a dependency you should notice in review, because it is an apply-order dependency between projects.
Document the contract next to the variables: units, whether null is allowed, and what the module will create. A variable named name that sometimes is a prefix is how two teams apply two different conventions into one account.

How Providers Work: Plugins, Authentication, and Configuration Blocks
Init downloads a binary and records a checksum in .terraform.lock.hcl. Plan and apply start that binary and speak the plugin protocol. The provider configuration is the settings every resource of that type inherits:
terraform {
  required_version = "~&gt; 1.13.0"

  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~&gt; 5.0"
    }
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  region = "eu-west-1"
}
Leave the access key out of the block. The AWS provider uses the standard credential chain: environment variables, a shared config file, or a role from the instance or the pipeline. Azure uses a service principal or workload identity. GCP uses application-default credentials or a workload identity pool. Chapter 12 lists those patterns as the security rule. The configuration above is the whole provider block when the environment already has credentials.
tofu providers shows what is required and what was installed. tofu providers lock updates the lock file for the platforms you name, so a developer on ARM64 and a pipeline on AMD64 do not fight over checksums. Lock the platforms you actually run.

Creating Resources Across Clouds: AWS, Azure, GCP Examples
These are sketches of the same idea: one named object, tags or labels from a variable, no embedded secrets. They will not apply until the matching provider is configured and the account exists. The pet project in chapter 3 is the one that applies as written.
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "state" {
  bucket = "example-tofu-state-203-0-113"
}

resource "azurerm_resource_group" "edge" {
  name     = "example-tofu-rg"
  location = "westeurope"
}

resource "google_storage_bucket" "state" {
  name     = "example-tofu-state-203-0-113"
  location = "EU"
}
The shape to copy is not the resource type. It is the discipline around it. The name is explicit, not generated from an unknown. The region is an input. Tags that mark the owning team and the state key are how a human in the console knows the object is managed. Deleting the block later deletes the object. If the object must survive, it does not belong in this configuration, or it belongs behind prevent_destroy with a written reason.
On-premises follows the same shape with a different provider: a hypervisor, a DNS server, a load balancer, an IPAM. If the provider is a community plugin, pin it harder and read who publishes it. The registry namespace is the publisher’s identity.

Data Sources: Querying Existing Infrastructure Safely
A data source is for an object you do not want this state to own.
data "aws_caller_identity" "current" {}

output "account_id" {
  value = data.aws_caller_identity.current.account_id
}
Use a data source when another team owns the object and you need its id. Do not use a data source to look up an object this same state created. Use that object’s attribute. A data source that searches by a moving filter (“the most recent image”) changes plans under you. Pin the identifier when the filter is only a convenience.
A data source that fails because the object is missing fails the plan. That is the right failure when the dependency is real. It is the wrong tool for “create this if it does not exist.” Existence-as-create is a resource.
depends_on on a data source is occasionally required when the object is created in the same configuration and the data source would otherwise read too early. If you need that, you are usually one refactor away from passing the attribute directly.

Provider Aliases: Managing Multiple Configurations Simultaneously
One default provider configuration is the unnamed block. An alias is a second configuration of the same provider, almost always for a second region or a second account.
provider "aws" {
  region = "eu-west-1"
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "backup"
  region = "eu-central-1"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "backup" {
  provider = aws.backup
  bucket   = "example-tofu-backup-203-0-113"
}
Resources use the default unless provider says otherwise. Modules do not inherit aliases. You pass them:
module "backup" {
  source = "./modules/bucket"
  providers = {
    aws = aws.backup
  }
}
The child declares configuration_aliases only when it must. A module that quietly creates objects in a region the caller did not pass is a bug. OpenTofu 1.9’s provider for_each is how you build one module instance per region from a map without copying the alias block by hand. The map keys must be static.

Remote State Configuration: Locking, Encryption, and Versioning
Locking makes a second apply wait or fail while the first holds the lock. Always turn it on. A backend that cannot lock is the wrong backend for production.
Versioning on the bucket, blob container, or GCS bucket keeps previous state objects. That is your backup when a bad apply writes a bad state. Lifecycle-expire old versions on a schedule you have tested a restore from. A versioned bucket nobody can read is not a backup.
Client-side encryption (OpenTofu 1.7 and later) encrypts the state and, if you ask, the plan, before they leave the process. The backend then stores ciphertext. A passphrase of at least 16 characters, fed from TF_VAR_, is the small-team form:
variable "state_passphrase" {
  type      = string
  sensitive = true
}

terraform {
  encryption {
    key_provider "pbkdf2" "main" {
      passphrase = var.state_passphrase
    }

    method "aes_gcm" "main" {
      keys = key_provider.pbkdf2.main
    }

    state {
      method   = method.aes_gcm.main
      enforced = true
    }

    plan {
      method   = method.aes_gcm.main
      enforced = true
    }
  }
}
enforced = true refuses to write plaintext. Lose the passphrase and the state is gone. Store the passphrase in the same secret manager the pipeline uses, and store a recovery copy under the same controls as any other encryption key. For a team, prefer a KMS key provider (aws_kms, gcp_kms, Azure Key Vault, or OpenBao) so access is IAM, not a shared password. Rotate by writing a new method and a fallback block that can still decrypt the old one. Take an unencrypted backup, offline and access-controlled, before the first migration onto encryption.

Reading Remote State: Cross-Referencing Infrastructure Between Projects
terraform_remote_state is a data source that reads another state and returns that state’s outputs.
data "terraform_remote_state" "network" {
  backend = "s3"

  config = {
    bucket = "example-tofu-state"
    key    = "network/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "eu-west-1"
  }
}

output "vpc_id" {
  value = data.terraform_remote_state.network.outputs.vpc_id
}
Only outputs are part of the contract. Reaching for anything else couples you to the other project’s resource names. The reader needs permission to read that state, which means the reader can also see every secret in it. Prefer passing an id through a narrow output over sharing a whole state with a wide team. Where the dependency is permanent, a data source on the real object (data "aws_vpc") is often a smaller permission than read access to someone else’s state.
Remote state creates an apply order. The network project must have been applied, and its outputs must exist, before the consumer plans. Write that order down. A pipeline that applies consumers in parallel with their producers will fail, or worse, will succeed against stale outputs.

Calling Modules: Local Paths, Registry Sources, and Git Repositories
module "pets" {
  source     = "./modules/pets"
  pet_length = var.pet_length
}

module "pets_remote" {
  source  = "example.com/team/pets/generic"
  version = "1.4.2"
}
A local path (./modules/pets) is the right source while the module and the root change together. There is no separate version. The git commit is the version.
A registry source has a version constraint. Pin it the way you pin providers. version = "~&gt; 1.4" accepts patch releases. Read them. An unbounded version is a supply-chain surprise.
A git source pins a ref:
module "pets_git" {
  source = "git::https://example.invalid/team/pets.git?ref=v1.4.2"
}
Pin a tag or a commit. Pinning a branch tracks whatever the branch points at the next time someone deletes .terraform/modules and inits. That is not reproducibility. Private git needs the pipeline’s credentials, not a token in the source URL.
OpenTofu 1.8 can use variables in module source in more cases than Terraform 1.5 could. A source that changes with a variable changes which code you are about to apply. Keep sources static in root modules unless you have a reviewed reason.

Advanced Module Patterns: Stacks, Composition, and Versioned Releases
Composition means a root module calls a few child modules and passes outputs across. That is the pattern to keep.
module "name" {
  source     = "./modules/pets"
  pet_length = 2
}

module "record" {
  source = "./modules/record"
  name   = module.name.name
}
A stack, in the sense that matters here, is that root plus its state plus its pipeline. It is not a new language. HashiCorp’s Terraform Stacks product is a separate commercial workflow. You do not need it to compose OpenTofu modules.
Versioned releases apply to modules other teams call. Tag semver. A changelog says whether inputs were added (compatible) or renamed (breaking). Internal modules used by one root can live in the same repository and move without tags until a second caller appears. The day a second caller appears, the module gets a version or it gets copied. A floating local path shared by two roots via a relative ../../ is how unrelated applies break together.
for_each on a module creates one instance per key, each with the same child code and different inputs. The keys are stable names. This is the right way to stamp out one bucket per application. It is the wrong way to hide a loop the reader cannot see without expanding state.

Lifecycle Arguments: create_before_destroy, prevent_destroy, ignore_changes
lifecycle is a meta-argument on a resource.
create_before_destroy = true creates the replacement before deleting the old object. Use it when the name can differ (so both can exist) and something else will retarget, such as a listener that follows the new instance. It fails when the identity must be unique, such as a fixed bucket name. The plan will tell you the create cannot proceed while the old object holds the name.
prevent_destroy = true makes any plan that would destroy the resource an error. Use it on the state bucket’s own configuration if that configuration lives in OpenTofu at all, and on data you cannot recreate. It also makes refactors louder, which is what you want. Removing the flag is a reviewed change, not a quick comment-out during an apply.
ignore_changes tells the plan to leave listed arguments alone even if the configuration and the world differ.
resource "random_pet" "service" {
  length = 2

  lifecycle {
    ignore_changes = [separator]
  }
}
Ignore a field when something outside OpenTofu is the writer of that field and you have accepted the split: an autoscaler setting capacity, a security tool setting a tag. Ignoring all with ignore_changes = all hides every drift on that resource. That is rarely what you meant. Ignoring an argument you set yourself hides your own edits. Take the argument out of the configuration if OpenTofu should not own it.
replace_triggered_by forces replacement when another value changes. Use it when a resource does not see an argument change that should have rebuilt it. Prefer fixing the argument over a trigger when the provider supports the change.

Importing Existing Resources: The import Command and Workflow
Import writes an existing remote object into state under an address. It does not write configuration. After a successful import, a plan with no configuration for that address wants to destroy the object. Write the configuration first, or generate it in the same step, and plan before you apply.
The declarative form, stable since the 1.5 language:
import {
  to = aws_s3_bucket.state
  id = "example-tofu-state-203-0-113"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "state" {
  bucket = "example-tofu-state-203-0-113"
}
tofu plan shows an import. tofu apply records the object in state. The next plan should be empty. If it is not, the configuration does not match the object and apply would change a legacy bucket. Fix the configuration until the plan is empty. That empty plan is the proof you imported rather than adopted-and-mutated.
The one-shot CLI tofu import aws_s3_bucket.state example-tofu-state-203-0-113 does the same state write without an import block. Prefer the block. It is reviewed, it can take for_each, and it documents the migration in git. Remove the import block after every state has applied it, the same way you remove a moved block.
The id string is provider-specific. For many resources it is the cloud id. For others it is a composite (name/namespace). Read the provider’s import document for that resource type. Guessing the id imports the wrong object or imports nothing.

OpenTofu Test Framework: Built-in Testing Capabilities
Tests are files named *.tftest.hcl. A run block executes plan or apply and asserts.
# tests/pet.tftest.hcl
run "two_words" {
  command = plan

  variables {
    pet_length = 2
  }

  assert {
    condition     = random_pet.service.length == 2
    error_message = "The pet resource did not receive length 2."
  }
}
tofu test from the module directory runs every file. A failing assert fails the command. Variables in the run override defaults. Mocking providers is available when a test would otherwise need a cloud API. Prefer a module boundary you can test with random or with pure locals over a large mock that restates the provider.
Test the contract: given this length, the resource is configured with this length, and the output is not null after an apply test. Do not test the provider’s implementation. HashiCorp’s and the provider’s own tests already do that, and a copy of them in your module fails for reasons you cannot fix.
tofu test is not a policy engine. An assert that “no security group allows 0.0.0.0/0” is policy that belongs on every repository, not only on modules that remembered to copy the test.
